<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <!-- THIS FIXTURE MUST NEVER COMMIT A LOCKFILE.

         It is the negative of testdata/dotnet-app, which sets
         RestorePackagesWithLockFile and commits packages.lock.json so the
         dependency scan has something to read. Here nothing opts in — which is
         what NuGet does by default, and therefore what a real consumer's
         repository looks like.

         On this tree Trivy resolves no .NET graph at all: measured on trivy
         0.70.0, the report holds no Results array, zero packages and zero
         vulnerabilities, byte-identical to a genuinely clean repository. That
         silence is the whole point — it is what the lockfile-free restore
         fallback (src/security/dotnetrestore.go) exists to turn into a real
         scan, and what the module-test cell asserts is no longer silent.

         So: do not add RestorePackagesWithLockFile, do not commit a
         packages.lock.json, and do not commit bin/ or obj/ (a *.deps.json in
         there is a graph Trivy reads, which would clear the check the fixture
         is built to trip). -->
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Intentionally old version with a known CVE. Nothing here is built,
         installed or shipped by this repository; see trivy.yaml, which skips
         testdata for exactly this reason. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="9.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
